---
import "../sternzeit/sternzeit.css";
import Try from "../Try.astro";
import Choice from "./Choice.astro";
import "./dunstkreis.css";
import "./wavelengths.css";
---

<figure class="strip-figure wide" id="wavelengths">
    <div class="wavelengths-bar">
        <Choice
            figure="wavelengths"
            name="preset"
            label="Wavelengths"
            title="Which wavelengths the sky is computed at, and how they become a color"
            options={[
                ["fitted", "dunstkreis’ four", "630, 580, 510 and 440 nm, turned into RGB by a fitted matrix"],
                ["samples", "Bruneton’s three", "680, 550 and 440 nm, taken as red, green and blue"],
            ]}
            checked="fitted"
        />
        {
            import.meta.env.DEV && (
                <Choice
                    figure="wavelengths"
                    name="variant"
                    label="Variant"
                    title="Draft only: the figure without and with a live sky"
                    options={[
                        ["A", "variant A"],
                        ["B", "variant B"],
                    ]}
                    checked="B"
                />
            )
        }
    </div>
    <div class="strip-panel wavelengths-strip">
        <svg aria-label="The visible spectrum, the eye's three weights over it, and the wavelengths, draggable"></svg>
    </div>
    <div class="wavelengths-skies" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Skies"></div>
    <div class="wavelengths-numbers">
        <p class="wavelengths-score"></p>
        <table class="wavelengths-matrix" aria-label="The matrix from the samples to linear sRGB"></table>
    </div>
    <div class="wavelengths-live as-seen" hidden>
        <div>
            <canvas data-field="reference" aria-label="The whole sky at the chosen moment, at dunstkreis' four wavelengths"></canvas>
            <span class="wavelengths-live-name">dunstkreis’ four</span>
        </div>
        <div>
            <canvas data-field="chosen" aria-label="The whole sky at the chosen moment, at the chosen wavelengths"></canvas>
            <span class="wavelengths-live-name" data-field="chosenName"></span>
        </div>
    </div>
    <figcaption class="figure-caption">
        The wavelengths a sky is computed at, on the visible spectrum under the eye's weights for linear red, green and
        blue, each a sum over the whole spectrum; shaded, the spectrum of the sky chosen below. Under it, eight skies,
        each as its true color, left, and the color from the samples alone, right, with how far apart they are: with
        four, by a matrix fitted to sky spectra, refitted at once; with Bruneton's three, the samples taken as they are.
        Underneath, the whole sky overhead at the chosen moment, the Sun at the bottom, at dunstkreis' four and at the
        chosen wavelengths.
        <Try label="Twilight over Potsdam" set="date=2026-04-06T18:20:00Z&lat=52.395&lon=13.085&h=1" fig="wavelengths">
            With Bruneton's three, the whole sky turns pink and violet, the blue band low opposite the Sun included.
        </Try>
    </figcaption>
</figure>

<script>
    import "./wavelengths";
</script>
